Fair enough. For me it would be Recharge Mode, though. The first round you play it, it just replaces itself, costing you your play and power (which could have been a draw anyway). It only does anything at all in subsequent rounds, and is really only good for loading up the Omni-Cannon.

[/quote]

I use Recharge Mode for tanking, card draw is just a side benefit. &nbsp;Especially nice in Vengeance mode where at least one Ongoing is likely to be wiped before Bunker&#39;s next turn anyhow so it&#39;s a great sacrifice card. &nbsp;Upgrade Mode is strictly discard fodder, always, so I like Powerhound&#39;s mod.

[quote="TakeWalker"]

I&#39;d like to take a moment to speak up in defense of Devastating Aurora.Having this card in one deck in the entire game is great. Make it an Ongoing, and it&#39;s Apocalypse, which I generally laugh at.But That Card? It&#39;s&nbsp;<em>That Card</em> for a reason! It&#39;s the kind of card legends are built around!You know what you&#39;re getting into against Dawn. Maybe she&#39;ll wreck your crew in the middle of the game, when you were just about to turn things to your favor. Maybe she&#39;ll play both in the first two rounds, setting you scott-free for the rest of the game. Maybe you&#39;ll never see it all... except for that one time Setback discards one off the top of her deck and you all breathe a sigh of relief and shake his hand for being so darn clumsy.Devastating Aurora is punishing, yes, but it&#39;s&nbsp;<em>exciting</em>. Plus, it&#39;s thematic that she destroys more stuff than Progeny because&nbsp;<em>who won in the Cosmic Contest?</em> That should&#39;ve been our clue. :VGranted, if there&#39;s a card I could change, it&#39;d be Sedative Flechettes. Tons of damage on top of a board wipe is just not fun. :/ Technological Singularity is nasty, but at least it&#39;s thematic. I&#39;d rather the Flechettes did, say, (H)-1 to all, or 3 to the (H)-1 highest, and destroy one ongoing for each hero dealt damage. A little bit of setup destruction keeps a villain from being, well, Ambuscade, but that card&#39;s ridiculous and I hate it.

[/quote]

Agreed. That card is one of my favorite villain cards! I like that you have to plan around it and it makes the fight unique. I don&#39;t mind Flechettes for similar reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

 My least favorite villain card is Spite&#39;s card that releases all the bystanders you have saved, making saving bystanders typically a bad play and defeating the purpose of the deck.&nbsp; 

But rather than trying to save Spite&#39;s deck, the one card I&#39;d change is probably speed loading or upgrade mode.&nbsp;

